I got two answers to my problem with the 'names' function. Thanks to: 

Vincent Furstoss
Patrick Connolly

Patrick suggested saving the column names in a separate vector and then
binding it with the other vectors of results, which is the temporary
solution I used until I got the final answer by Vincent. He suggested to
add the command line

res[,"sp"] <- I(as.character(res[,"sp"]))

right after the 

res[k,"sp"]_names(dataframe)[i] command

to convert the sp column in the output dataframe into a character mode. 
And it works perfectly fine !

Hence, once more, thanks to this great list !

Below is the full answer by Vincent, translated from French.
My original question is pasted at the bottom of the mail.

-------------Vincent's answer----------------------
Try the following:
  sp.name_names(dataframe)[i]  # double brackets are not necessary
  res[k,"sp"]_sp.name
        # the "sp" column of res becomes a factor
        # it must be converted into character mode
        # to be able to complete it
  res[,"sp"] <- I(as.character(res[,"sp"]))

Here is what happens:

> df<-data.frame()
> df[1,"c1"] <- "pipo"
> is.factor(df[,"c1"])
[1] T
> df[2,"c1"] <- "rien"
Warning messages:
  replacement values not all in levels(x): NA's generated in:
"[<-.factor"(xj,
        iseq, value = vjj)
> df
    c1 
1 pipo
2   NA

# hence, the following is necessary:
>  df<-data.frame()
>  df[1,"c1"] <- "pipo"
>  is.factor(df[,"c1"])
>  df[,"c1"] <- I(as.character(df[,"c1"]))
>  is.factor(df[,"c1"])
[1] F
>  df[2,"c1"] <- "rien"
> df
    c1 
1 pipo
2 rien

# Here are the explanations
# it is the default behaviour of the data.frame function
# which is rather difficult to understand and caused problems here
